// A user type takes a prelude name the prelude ITSELF uses, and the prelude
// stops type-checking.
//
// The reference prepends its prelude to the program and checks the two together,
// so a declaration that removes `Vec` leaves the prelude's own `Vec<T>` looking
// at a type with no parameters. The program's verdict is that failure, reported
// at the head of the prelude. The port synthesises its prelude on demand and
// never asked, so it compiled a program the reference refuses - for every name
// the prelude writes.
//
// The verdict follows the DECLARATION, not any use: this file uses nothing.
struct Vec { a: int }

fn main() { println(1); }
